action.message:
Implemented the fix for "__in doesn't clear selected fields on the RHS when QuerySet.alias() is used after annotate()".

Root cause: `Query.has_select_fields` returned `True` whenever `annotation_select_mask` was set. `alias()` sets that mask (to exclude the alias from SELECT), so a queryset built with `.annotate(...).alias(...)` was treated as having explicit select fields. As a result, `RelatedIn.get_prep_lookup` skipped `clear_select_clause()`, leaving all model columns plus annotations in the subquery SELECT, producing "sub-select returns N columns - expected 1".

Fix (django/db/models/sql/query.py): `has_select_fields` now only counts annotations as explicit select fields when they are the actual output, i.e. when `default_cols` is False (as set by `values()`). A plain `annotate()`/`alias()` chain keeps `default_cols = True`, so the RHS is correctly cleared to the target field.

```python
@property
def has_select_fields(self):
    return bool(
        self.select
        or self.extra_select_mask
        or (self.annotation_select_mask and not self.default_cols)
    )
```

This preserves `.values('annotation')` behavior (default_cols is False there) while fixing the annotate+alias subquery case. Public regression suite (annotations) passes: 80 tests, 0 failures.

